9289 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129289 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129290 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1045 "The Parallel Axis Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1050 1440.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129370 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c10a40bf-815b-59d3-8b54-688b2275327e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129548 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f04d615-f6b4-5013-9889-65ecf75e2ffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129548 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{0+2}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6(x-1)^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2(x-1)^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4-(-2) \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =8+{2}^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =8+48 \\\\  & =56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129693 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "424a10e5-7557-50d9-8260-a7eb80c40d48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129693 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ its center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}=r.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{15}+\\frac{10\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1061 43340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aae6c762-bad6-59ad-bbac-4dd3947ca34e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1061 43341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a371a864-c197-5aa0-8d21-3cead8839e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1061 43342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f9433b3-3716-5cab-88dc-0c8106c423cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130249 #edb/ref 1061 44997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130249 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "942eea0b-0401-5ec3-980b-a17bb6c3cdaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130249 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130250 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130250 #edb/ref 1061 45003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5790220-0fbc-5caf-b3ee-9039c4289a67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130262 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130262 #edb/ref 1061 45317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130262 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f23fbe74-8ade-5fcf-9513-af6482f642b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130262 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130459 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130459 #edb/ref 1065 7332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130459 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b1dda94b-0ea5-5ff2-a3d5-a406dfc9b95d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130459 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130480 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that the moment of inertia about an axis $l$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D [r(x,y)]^2\\,\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of $D$ about the line through the center of mass $\\left(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y}\\right)$ and perpendicular to the lamina is given by\n\n$$\nI_M = \\iint\\limits_D \\left((x-\\bar{x})^2+(y-\\bar{y})^2\\right) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130480 #edb/ref 1065 7849 #edb/r